  "merits": "Abdullah, son of Abd al-Muttalib, was the father of the Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) and a brother of Abu Talib, the father of Amir al-Mu'minin (a.s.). Abd al-Muttalib had vowed to sacrifice one of his sons; the lot fell on Abdullah, and he ransomed him with a hundred camels. So the Prophet (s.a.w.a.) said, \"I am the son of the two who were offered for sacrifice\": Isma'il and Abdullah. Abdullah died in Yathrib (Madinah) on his way back from a trading journey to Syria, before the Prophet (s.a.w.a.) was born by the better-known account, and was buried in Dar al-Nabigha. His grave stood near the old market, opposite Bab al-Salam of the Prophet's Mosque; the place is now part of the mosque's prayer ground, and its exact spot is no longer known. Shia scholars hold that all the Prophet's forefathers, back to Adam (a.s.), believed in the One God, as this ziyarat bears witness.\n\nBoth forms are given in the Shia manuals of Hajj, such as Sayyid Jawad Husayni Shahrudi's Adab al-Haramayn, without a narrated source."
